               <head>Treatises on heraldry; England, second half 15th century</head>
               <msContents>
                  <msItem n="1" xml:id="MS_Lat_misc_e_86-item1">
                     <locus from="1r" to="32r">(fols. 1r-32r)</locus>
                     <author key="person_60506408">Johannes de Bado Aureo</author>
                     <title key="work_2540">Tractatus de armis</title>
                     <incipit>Quoniam de armis multociens in clipeis depictis</incipit>
                     <explicit>Et hec de armis et insigniis dixisse sufficiat</explicit>
                     <finalRubric>Explicit nobilis tractatus de armis</finalRubric>
                     <note>Here without title. Ed. E. J. Jones, <title>Medieval Heraldry</title>, Cardiff, 1943, pp.95–143. An English translation is in MS. Laud Misc. 733.</note>
                     <!--<note>A number of coats are also blazoned in French as usual</note>-->
                     <textLang mainLang="la" otherLangs="xno">Latin with a little Anglo-Norman</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem>
                     <locus from="32v" to="32v">(fol. 32v)</locus>
                     <title type="desc">Five coats of arms with blazons in English.</title>
                     <incipit><note>[first coat]</note> He bereth siluer barre goules ij contercheuerons of the same asur a cheueron sable iij roses goules persed in golde iij faus schochons of the same. </incipit>
                     <explicit><note>[last coat]</note> Rex <unclear>orcianen’</unclear> golde ij sarasen hedes sable crowned goules vppon ij ragged staves sable crossy.</explicit>
                     <note>The first and second coats are anonymous; the third and fourth are 'rex bougis - siluer a pelet goules', 'rex salamynis - golde iiij bendes goules'. Fol. 33r-v is blank.</note>
                     <textLang mainLang="enm">Middle English</textLang>
                  </msItem>
                  <msItem xml:id="MS_Lat_misc_e_86-item2">
                     <locus from="34r" to="42v" >(fols. 34r-42v)</locus>
                     <title key="work_15057">Treatise on heraldry (‘The Ashmolean Tract’ or ‘John's Treatise’)</title>
                     <incipit>First as herowdis recorden atte siege of Troye within the towne and without for the nobilnes of the dedes that were shewed on bothe parties
                     and for as moche as ther was so huge multitude of people that oon might not be knowen from another of their poyntes of worship</incipit>
                     <explicit>And if he bere in thus wise [diagram] he berith siluer and goules belly - he berith silver [diagram] - he berith siluer [diagram] </explicit>
                     <note>Appears to break off incomplete, missing the final words given in CUL Dd.10.52. Fol. 43r-v is blank.</note>
                     <listBibl>
                        <bibl>Wells Rev. 10:3930 [497] lists nine manuscripts, not including this one. Cf. IMEP 9 indexing BodL Ashmole 15(A) (= MS. Ash. Rolls 4) [1] and IMEP 19 indexing CUL Dd.10.52 [1]
                        </bibl>
                        <bibl>IPMEP 223 (with incipit from the prologue which appears uniquely in BL Add. MS 34648)</bibl>
                        <bibl>Pr. from MS. Ash. Rolls 4 by C. R. Humphery-Smith, 'Heraldry in School Manuals of the Middle Ages. II. The Ashmolean Tract', <title>Coat of Arms</title> 6 (1960-1), 163-170 </bibl>
                        <bibl>Jones, <title>Heraldry</title>, 213-220, prints versions from BL Add. MS 34648 and Harl. 6097 with variations in order and in the material covered. </bibl>
                     </listBibl>
                     <!--<note>Found also in MS. Ashmole Roll 4 (cf. H. S. London, <title>Antiquaries Journal</title> 33 (1953), 171). For F. P. Bernard's transcriptions of this text see MS. Eng. misc. c. 152.</note>-->
                    
                     <textLang mainLang="enm">Middle English</textLang>
                  </msItem>
               </msContents>
